<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">British Virgin Islands</h1><p class="st-meta">1672 CE – present · capital Road Town</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">British island territory east of Puerto Rico, taken from the Dutch in 1672, once home to Quaker planters and now a major centre of offshore companies.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> buccaneers and settlers had a foothold on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tortola">Tortola</a> when English forces from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leeward-islands">Leeward Islands</a> seized it in 1672. Over the following decades English settlers spread to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virgin-gorda">Virgin Gorda</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jost-van-dyke">Jost Van Dyke</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anegada">Anegada</a>, and the islands were formally attached to the Leeward Islands government.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>A small planter society grew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> with enslaved labour; an 18th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quakers">Quaker</a> community on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tortola">Tortola</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jost-van-dyke">Jost Van Dyke</a> produced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-coakley-lettsom">John Coakley Lettsom</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-thornton">William Thornton</a>, architect of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states-capitol">United States Capitol</a>. An elected assembly existed from 1773 but lapsed in 1902 as the economy declined after emancipation. Many islanders turned to fishing, smallholding and work in the neighbouring Danish and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states-virgin-islands">American Virgin Islands</a>.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leeward-islands">Leeward Islands</a> federation ended, the BVI became a separate colony in 1960 and gained ministerial government in 1967. They did not seek independence. Tourism, yachting and, from 1984, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/international-business-companies-act-british-virgin-islands">International Business Companies Act</a> made them prosperous, and they remain a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-overseas-territories">British Overseas Territory</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/british-virgin-islands.jpg" alt="British Virgin Islands" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: No machine-readable author provided. Dbenbenn assumed (based on copyright claims). · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_British_Virgin_Islands.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1672 CE – present</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Road Town</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Methodism and Anglicanism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>English</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>British Virgin Islands</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Virgin Islands (British)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1849&amp;focus=british-virgin-islands" aria-label="British Virgin Islands on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2929 1494 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where British Virgin Islands lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M3249 1750L4997 651M3249 1750L3320 1799"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4997 651h0M3320 1799h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3249 1750h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3249 1750h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Road Town. <a href="/atlas?year=1849&amp;focus=british-virgin-islands">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

